Problem 1 — Port 5000 Already in Use

What Happened

Flask started correctly.

Instead of stopping Flask with:

Ctrl+C

the process was suspended using:

Ctrl+Z

Shell output showed:

Stopped flask --app app.app run

When Flask was started again:

Address already in use
Port 5000 is in use by another program.

Root Cause

Ctrl+Z suspends a Linux process.

It does not terminate it.

The suspended Flask process still owned port 5000.

Flask owns port 5000
        ↓
Ctrl+Z
        ↓
Flask suspended
        ↓
Process still exists
        ↓
Port remains occupied
        ↓
Second Flask server cannot bind to port 5000

Troubleshooting Commands

Check shell jobs:

jobs

Bring suspended process back:

fg %1

Stop correctly:

Ctrl+C

Lesson Learned

Ctrl+C
→ terminate foreground process

Ctrl+Z
→ suspend foreground process

This was also useful Linux process-management practice.

