Session 4 — Health Endpoint

Goal

Create an application endpoint that can later support Docker and Kubernetes health checks.

Added:

@app.route("/health")
def health():
    return {"status": "healthy"}, 200

Test

curl http://127.0.0.1:5000/health

Result:

{"status":"healthy"}

Header test:

curl -I http://127.0.0.1:5000/health

Result included:

HTTP/1.1 200 OK

Why /health Exists

It answers:

Is the application process alive?

Future Kubernetes mapping:

livenessProbe
      ↓
GET /health
      ↓
200 OK

If liveness fails repeatedly, Kubernetes can restart the container.

Session 5 — Readiness Endpoint

Added:

@app.route("/ready")
def ready():
    return {"status": "ready"}, 200

Test

curl http://127.0.0.1:5000/ready

Result:

{"status":"ready"}

Liveness vs Readiness

Liveness

Question:

Is the process alive?

Endpoint:

/health

Readiness

Question:

Is the application ready to receive traffic?

Endpoint:

/ready

Future example after PostgreSQL is introduced:

Flask process running
    ↓
/health → 200

PostgreSQL unavailable
    ↓
/ready → 503

Kubernetes could keep the Pod alive while removing it from Service traffic.

